# Conventional Commits

Format: `type(scope): description`

- **type** (one, lowercase): `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `perf`, `style`, `build`, `ci`
- **scope**: always required, short, lowercase kebab-case (e.g. `tenancy`, `posts`, `auth`, `ci`)
- **description**: short, clear, lowercase, no trailing punctuation, one logical change

```text
✅ feat(posts): add scheduled publishing job
✅ fix(tenancy): pin central user model to central connection
✅ ci(github): run tests and build on pull requests
❌ feat: implement stuff            (missing scope)
❌ Fix(Posts): Added Things.         (uppercase, past tense, punctuation)
❌ feat(posts): add covers and fix ci (two unrelated changes)
```

## Workflow

1. Inspect `git status` and `git diff` before committing.
2. Stage only the intended files; split unrelated changes into separate commits.
3. Never stage `.env`, secrets, `vendor/`, `node_modules/`, or tenant data.
4. Never add `Co-Authored-By` or AI-attribution lines.
5. Only commit or push when explicitly asked, and confirm success from git output.
